  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nDeep layer ridging is increasing in intensity overhead as our\nafternoon temperatures once again approach 90 degrees. The flip\nto the sharply amplified ridging in the east is the large\nswirling closed low/remnants of Tropical Cyclone Polo over the\nsouthwestern US. As that system gets picked up by the westerly\nlarge scale flow our rain chances increase Thursday night,\npeaking on Friday as a cold front pushes through the area. Owing\nto its warm core roots the mid-level lapse rates with this\nsystem are quite poor which holds instability back despite\nfairly rich boundary layer (and column) moisture and heat ahead\nof the front. \n\nA few thunderstorms appear possible but most of\nthe activity looks to be showers or areas of broad stratiform\nrain. QPF totals are holding steady with an areal average\nbetween a half inch and an inch persisting in guidance this\nafternoon. Overall instability looks too limited for much of a\nsevere thunderstorm threat. 2.00 inch PWAT values and deep warm\ncloud layer depth point towards very efficient rainfall rates\nbut forcing/intensity still appears too modest for much flooding\nrisk (probably about a 5-10% chance for flash flooding somewhere\nin the CWA) particularly given the fairly dry antecedent \nconditions.\n\nBehind the front we end up with highs in the mid 70s. A\nsecondary shot of drier and cooler air is sent this way Monday\ninto Tuesday which looks to re-open the window to overnight lows\nin the upper 40s Monday night.",
